{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791432145,"nonce":"BvLuo2vEqugodHBR9Y9jsIIWZAQ=","message":"The v5 probes disambiguate three naming and ABI failures. RAYZOR_ENUM_LOOKUP_DEBUG shows the original enum helper HIR arguments include a leading Type receiver. That receiver resolves to haxe.macro.Type, and hidden enum-id extraction selected it instead of the enum values. Skipping named type receivers and recognizing Type.typeof calls with a receiver fixes this inference, but the enum identity regression then fails on null.\n\nThe resulting MIR uses hidden id 443883353, the hash of enum:Type.ValueType. ValueType was registered before the explicit Type.ValueType import, under the declaration name ValueType (hash 411938711). The import fallback mutates the declaration qualified_name because lookup_module_subtype rejects two-segment paths. A standalone metadata probe consequently prints null for Type.getEnumName(ValueType).\n\nRAYZOR_RESOLVE_TRACE also shows the original root Type symbol (26) has been changed into an enum during macro dependency registration. A named-package enum can claim an unpackaged class placeholder, and root declarations were never given an explicit package identity. The next experiment registers default-package declarations in the root namespace, distinguishes declared root classes from unresolved placeholders, and allows two-segment module subtype imports.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/rayzor-enum-reflection-lookup-v5.log, /tmp/rayzor-enum-reflection-mir-v5.log, /tmp/rayzor-enum-metadata-v5.log, /tmp/rayzor-enum-metadata-resolve-v5.log.\n\nReflectionTyping.hx passes native Haxe and Rayzor v5. EnumReflection.hx still fails, so no checkpoint is being claimed for this prototype.","files":null}]}